# Git commands: use full permissions

When using the **terminal / shell tool** for **any** git operation (`git add`, `git commit`, `git push`, `git pull`, `git fetch`, `git status`, `git log`, `git diff`, etc.), **always** request **full permissions** (run outside the sandbox; in Cursor, pass the equivalent of `required_permissions: ["all"]` for that command).

**Why:** Sandboxed or restricted runs can produce **empty or misleading output**, skip **hooks**, block **network** (breaking `push` / `fetch`), or interfere with **credential helpers**. If a git command appears to do nothing or `main` stays ahead of `origin` after `push`, **retry once with full permissions** before reporting failure.

This does **not** override collaboration rules: still **do not** commit or push until the project owner has tested and asked you to (see `.cursor/SETTINGS.md`).
